1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:00,810
All right.

2
00:00:00,890 --> 00:00:01,910
Well, thank you very much.

3
00:00:01,990 --> 00:00:10,630
It's a great honor to be here, and to be in the Royal Society, and to be speaking to all of you.

4
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:16,170
And today, I'm going to tell you about protein design.

5
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:32,610
And what I want to emphasize today is that protein design really gives, for the first time in science and technology, we can position atoms in space very precisely, and quite a large number of atoms.

6
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:43,150
And as I'll show you, we can do this by designing proteins which fold up in such a way to position the atoms that we're interested in exactly where we want them to.

7
00:00:43,662 --> 00:00:48,010
So this gives us really a new way to program matter at the atomic scale.

8
00:00:48,010 --> 00:01:03,270
So I want to tell you briefly how we do this, and then give you a series of examples which show you sort of the wide range of applications that this new capability brings.

9
00:01:04,334 --> 00:01:11,450
And this is all going to be done using, we're using generative AI methods to do this.

10
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:19,770
And I think there's a lot of concern about AI now in a lot of areas, and this is one of the good applications of dead AI.

11
00:01:20,174 --> 00:01:25,770
So I usually, when people, the media wants to talk to me, I'm always the token person doing something good with AI.

12
00:01:26,830 --> 00:01:43,570
So the generative AI methods that we have been developing are based, we find inspiration in the computer vision area, at least initially.

13
00:01:43,570 --> 00:02:10,650
And so, if you take all the images on the internet and you train a model and you noise them to different extents, and you train a model to remove the noise, you can then, starting with a completely random collection of pixels, like on the top, progressively denoise it to generate something that looks like an image from your training set, the cat sitting on the table, but it's completely new, in fact.

14
00:02:10,702 --> 00:02:23,450
And actually, Joe Watson, who's here, and colleagues found a number of years ago that you could do the same thing with proteins to noise them to different extents and then train a network to remove the noise.

15
00:02:23,990 --> 00:02:34,550
And then once the network had been trained, you could start with completely random collections of amino acid residues and progressively to noise to generate a new structure.

16
00:02:35,182 --> 00:02:39,310
Now that was RF diffusion one, then there was RFD2 and RFD3.

17
00:02:39,310 --> 00:02:43,610
And now we're on to RFD4, Protina, which I'll say a little bit about later.

18
00:02:44,010 --> 00:02:46,090
Kieran, who's here, is a major developer of that.

19
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:51,450
And the capabilities have gotten more and more sophisticated, and I'll allude to that as I go along.

20
00:02:52,990 --> 00:02:58,590
Now just generating a new image or a new protein structure alone isn't very useful.

21
00:02:58,894 --> 00:03:03,630
In the case of an image, you want to be able to say something like generate an image of a cat sitting on a table.

22
00:03:04,110 --> 00:03:07,530
In the case of proteins, you want to generate a protein that has a function.

23
00:03:07,530 --> 00:03:16,850
And so I'm going to show you today how we condition this generation process to make proteins that have a wide range of different functions.

24
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:20,590
So I'll first talk about applications in medicine.

25
00:03:21,294 --> 00:03:27,990
And on the left, you see in gray a receptor that plays a major role in inflammation.

26
00:03:27,990 --> 00:03:29,670
It's called the TNF receptor.

27
00:03:30,062 --> 00:03:35,050
And many of the drugs used to combat autoimmune disease target this receptor.

28
00:03:35,822 --> 00:03:51,010
And in the colors, you see an RF diffusion trajectory that is starting from a random collection of residues and progressively denoising it to generate a protein which is predicted to bind the receptor.

29
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:59,590
And Matheus Gogol, when he made this protein, found that, in fact, did bind the receptor very tightly with single-digit picomolar affinity.

30
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:05,150
And it was quite effective in animal models of inflammation.

31
00:04:05,530 --> 00:04:11,530
And so Mantis is currently developing this further as a therapeutic for autoimmune disease.

32
00:04:14,090 --> 00:04:19,810
Now we've since designed proteins for many different types of protein targets.

33
00:04:20,302 --> 00:04:27,190
In yellow is a G-protein coupled receptor, one of the major classes of proteins that has been targeted by drugs.

34
00:04:27,598 --> 00:04:32,590
And Idan Morozovic designed the protein shown on top in gray and blue.

35
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:35,876
And this protein binds tightly to the receptor.

36
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:43,970
And we have very encouraging results in stem cell mobilization, which is an important part in current adoptive cell therapies.

37
00:04:45,998 --> 00:04:53,230
Now an important class of proteins that have been very challenging to develop drugs for are intrinsically disordered proteins.

38
00:04:53,710 --> 00:04:58,170
And Jane Clark, who's here, really pioneered the study of these proteins.

39
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:00,930
They're hard to design.

40
00:05:01,870 --> 00:05:04,770
Small molecules are the things to bind because they don't have a defined structure.

41
00:05:05,390 --> 00:05:22,650
The way that we approach these is to allow them, the target here is shown in dark here, we allow it to sample different conformations at the same time we're designing a protein to bind to it, which is shown in the reddish color.

42
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:28,410
So, the disordered protein sort of gets folded up at the same time.

43
00:05:28,558 --> 00:05:30,150
It's kind of an induced fit mechanism.

44
00:05:30,710 --> 00:05:39,050
And Kaixuan Liu and Kejia Wu have used this approach to design binders to many different proteins, as well as Hana Han, who's here.

45
00:05:40,942 --> 00:05:52,310
And so, one of the, Kaixuan and Hana have shown that these proteins which bind, oh sorry, They've been designing proteins to bind to tau.

46
00:05:52,690 --> 00:05:59,850
Tau is one of the major contributors or proteins that's been implicated in Alzheimer's disease and other neurodegenerative diseases.

47
00:06:00,814 --> 00:06:10,930
So they have designed proteins which bind to parts of tau, which are particularly important in the amyloid forming process.

48
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:15,590
And one of these segments is shown in red there, and in blue is a protein which binds to it.

49
00:06:15,982 --> 00:06:18,170
So, these proteins have kind of remarkable properties.

50
00:06:18,170 --> 00:06:26,550
If they're added to fibrils of tau, so tau forms amyloid that's associated with disease, the fibrils come apart as you see on the top.

51
00:06:26,830 --> 00:06:37,210
And if the binding proteins are fused to a motif which directs proteins for degradation, it completely clears the tau from a cell.

52
00:06:37,350 --> 00:06:41,650
So on the on the right you see a cell that's expressing tau That's that black band.

53
00:06:41,934 --> 00:06:46,930
If you make this design with a degradation tag, it completely removes the tau from the cell.

54
00:06:47,246 --> 00:06:53,070
So we're very excited now about exploring therapeutic potential of this to neurodegenerative disease.

55
00:06:54,766 --> 00:06:59,870
Now, early detection is important in Alzheimer's and other neurodegenerative diseases.

56
00:07:00,206 --> 00:07:07,410
And one of the hallmarks is a modification of the tau protein, the addition of a phosphate, which is shown in red on the left.

57
00:07:07,822 --> 00:07:24,970
And Keiji Wu has been working on design of proteins which can recognize not only the sequence of tau, and that's sort of shown by the light green sticks, those are, that's a design, part of a design protein which recognizes the tau sequence, as well as the phosphate which is shown in red.

58
00:07:25,358 --> 00:07:33,670
And using these proteins, she's able to detect very specifically the modified tau in patient samples, that's in the left column.

59
00:07:33,670 --> 00:07:38,710
and patients who don't have the disease form of tau do not give a signal.

60
00:07:38,850 --> 00:07:41,630
That's sort of the bright color that's showing here.

61
00:07:42,090 --> 00:07:50,150
And by comparison, the antibodies that are currently used are not quite as good at specifically reading out both the sequence and the presence of the phosphate.

62
00:07:52,430 --> 00:08:00,050
So a lot of what we've been doing in this area is to go beyond binding to conditionality, to proteins that switch.

63
00:08:00,430 --> 00:08:16,510
So in medicine, a major problem with putting in a protein for example, for cancer that activates the immune system, or for autoimmune disease that dampens the immune system, the problem is you don't want that protein on everywhere, because it'll cause toxic side effects.

64
00:08:16,846 --> 00:08:21,230
That's, for example, in cancer chemotherapy.

65
00:08:22,286 --> 00:08:31,150
And so we've been, and the binding problem with the methods we and others have developed is becoming more closer to being solved.

66
00:08:31,150 --> 00:08:43,110
So we've really been focusing in the last couple of years, and almost everything I'm telling you about is from the last year or two, on designing switchable proteins that only bind to their target under certain conditions.

67
00:08:43,982 --> 00:08:46,870
So I'll first illustrate the application of this to medicine.

68
00:08:48,370 --> 00:08:56,230
And so here, this is a design protein shown in gray, which brings two receptor subunits together.

69
00:08:56,230 --> 00:09:02,750
the components of the IL-2 receptor, and that induces a very strong signal that activates the immune system.

70
00:09:04,238 --> 00:09:11,350
So Adam Brouwerman, who did this work, also designed, this is not just a simple binding protein, it has an on and off switch.

71
00:09:11,694 --> 00:09:23,850
So the yellow protein, which binds, actually turns this system off, and it does so, as you can see here, by causing a conformational change in the binding protein that then pushes the two receptors apart.

72
00:09:24,814 --> 00:09:39,670
And if you look at the signaling listed by adding the protein, you see that if you look at the blue curve on the right, when you add just the gray protein, signaling is activated, but then when the off switch is added, the signal turns off, and that's in yellow.

73
00:09:40,190 --> 00:09:50,550
So this is then a powerful way for introducing a protein which has a function into the body or elsewhere, and then being able to turn it off when you don't want it to work anymore.

74
00:09:50,550 --> 00:09:51,470
I'm

75
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:54,880
sorry.

76
00:09:57,880 --> 00:10:12,740
So Will Grubby did the same thing, did a similar, it was a similar idea, but now in the context of cancer, what you really want to do is activate the immune system in the vicinity of the tumor, but not throughout the body.

77
00:10:13,454 --> 00:10:32,440
And so he targeted a tumor marker, an integrin shown in orange here, and he designed a protein which is shown in the purple and blue and light blue, which has an immune-activating protein, an interferon, that's caged, it can't do anything.

78
00:10:32,782 --> 00:10:48,580
But when this integrin is present on the surface of a tumor cell, then you can see from the diagram that turns the switch and releases the signaling component of this, the purple part, to activate the receptor.

79
00:10:48,580 --> 00:10:50,500
And this works very well.

80
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:59,760
You can see some of the data on the right where, in the absence of this integrin, the thing that's flipping the switch.

81
00:11:00,174 --> 00:11:05,580
you need much higher concentration, that's further to the right, to activate the response.

82
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:11,700
whereas in the absence, when that's present, that's the curve on the right in blue.

83
00:11:12,110 --> 00:11:13,700
then there's a strong activation.

84
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:21,500
So, this is an on-off switch that's now dependent on something that's present on the tumor, so the drug is only active in the vicinity of the tumor.

85
00:11:22,960 --> 00:11:28,640
And Ho Jung Shin has been targeting cancer in a different way.

86
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:37,600
So in the vicinity of tumors, in the tumor microenvironment, there are proteases, proteins that cut protein chains, that aren't present elsewhere.

87
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:48,300
And so she designed an activating molecule, an IL-21 mimic, and a domain that masks this so it's not active at all.

88
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:54,840
But when it's in the presence of the tumor, the mask gets cut off and so now it is active.

89
00:11:55,310 --> 00:11:56,920
And this works very well.

90
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:06,400
This is in a mouse cancer model where this is a pancreatic cancer model, which is a very difficult cancer to treat.

91
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:12,380
So, if nothing is added, in black you see the tumor volume increasing quite a bit.

92
00:12:12,718 --> 00:12:19,360
If she adds the masked version or the unmasked version, that keeps the tumor in check.

93
00:12:20,238 --> 00:12:27,220
But if you look down below, the always-on version, the unmasked version, makes the animals very sick.

94
00:12:27,340 --> 00:12:29,760
They lose weight, whereas this masked one does not.

95
00:12:30,126 --> 00:12:39,300
So this is, by restricting immune activation to the vicinity of the tumor, this is retaining efficacy but removing the systemic toxicity.

96
00:12:41,230 --> 00:12:45,560
So we can also use this switch idea for controlling biological systems.

97
00:12:46,700 --> 00:12:52,600
And the example of this I'm going to give you is calcium flows, which are really important in the nervous system.

98
00:12:52,760 --> 00:12:54,640
So that's how neurons get activated.

99
00:12:55,220 --> 00:13:02,560
And so it's been very interesting to neurophysiologists to be able to activate an immune system.

100
00:13:02,894 --> 00:13:10,640
So, this year's Nobel Prize to Karl Deisseroth was given for being able to activate and record signaling with light.

101
00:13:11,020 --> 00:13:13,100
So, in this case, we have a...

102
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:24,680
This is work by Chuan Zhu, who designed a channel that's shown in gray there that opens in the presence of a flip-switching protein.

103
00:13:25,230 --> 00:13:27,180
And that, you can see the opening on the right.

104
00:13:27,180 --> 00:13:40,980
Now, this is exciting because it gives neurophysiologists and neurobiologists a way of turning on calcium flows where and when they want it during the development and function of the nervous system.

105
00:13:41,198 --> 00:13:48,000
by having one cell make this synthetic channel in gray and another cell nearby, make the activator.

106
00:13:48,140 --> 00:13:50,440
So only in that context will this channel open.

107
00:13:51,982 --> 00:14:05,040
And the final example I want to give you is from Tabitha Chow, who's, this is a great collaboration with Mark Banfield's lab here in the UK, and I have to thank Joe Watson again for initiating this collaboration.

108
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:19,740
And so what Tabitha did was to design a protein which has sort of a plant innate immune module shown in blue that's masked until a pathogen protein is present.

109
00:14:19,740 --> 00:14:27,160
And when that pathogen protein or pathogen protein is present, then the design protein actually kills the cell.

110
00:14:28,160 --> 00:14:31,820
And the Banfield lab has shown this works really well.

111
00:14:32,220 --> 00:14:43,960
So if in the when the pathogen protein is added, there's there's no it has no effect if it's just the protein alone on you see a leaf with and without without and with pathogen.

112
00:14:44,340 --> 00:14:53,420
But when this design protein is in the plant cell, that part of the leaf dies, which is exactly what you want the plant to do in the presence of the pathogen, so it can't replicate.

113
00:14:56,600 --> 00:15:00,200
So that's one of the things we've been working on hard in the last couple of years.

114
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:06,040
Another area is catalysis, making and breaking chemical bonds.

115
00:15:07,460 --> 00:15:12,000
And a lot of this work has been a great collaboration with Anthony Green's lab, who's here.

116
00:15:12,220 --> 00:15:15,280
And I'll tell you about some of the work done by Florence Hardy in just a sec.

117
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:22,380
So, the basic idea here, I told you about positioning atoms in space.

118
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:30,080
So in this case, the atoms we want to position in space on the left are the atoms that are going to carry out the chemical reaction.

119
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:39,000
And the way we position them in space is by building a protein around those atoms to hold them in exactly the right place that is needed for catalysis.

120
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,420
And so, Kiran Didius here has.

121
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:48,860
I mentioned we've been developing progressively improved generative methods over the years.

122
00:15:49,240 --> 00:15:58,940
and the most recent version called RFD4-Protina, developed in collaboration with NVIDIA, has some interesting properties.

123
00:15:59,180 --> 00:16:11,360
like inference with LLMs, you can change the amount of compute that's using, essentially, by changing the amount of reasoning.

124
00:16:11,520 --> 00:16:14,720
So we have low thinking, medium thinking, and high thinking versions.

125
00:16:15,120 --> 00:16:16,160
And it's very powerful.

126
00:16:17,960 --> 00:16:20,240
And so I'll show you some examples of using it.

127
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:22,080
This is now freely available.

128
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:30,640
And most of what, if you want to do the kinds of things that I'm describing in this talk, you can either write to me directly, we can collaborate, or you can use this software.

129
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:43,480
So a first example of a bond breaking reaction is organophosphate compounds, which are pesticides, insecticides, nerve agents.

130
00:16:43,566 --> 00:16:46,140
So there's a lot of reasons to want to break them down.

131
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:56,040
And they all have this structure that's shown on the top left with a phosphate group that you want to dissociate from the rest of it.

132
00:16:56,120 --> 00:16:57,880
This particular compound is paraxone.

133
00:16:58,318 --> 00:17:06,400
So, again, we start with idealized active site, in this case, computed with quantum chemistry methods.

134
00:17:06,839 --> 00:17:13,339
We then generate proteins around that site that hold the residues in the place.

135
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:23,060
And this is one of the exciting recent results with RFD4, that many of these designs were very active, you know, basically on the first shot.

136
00:17:23,182 --> 00:17:33,940
So we're very excited about developing these further to make proteins to help get rid of toxins in the environment and protect from nerve agents and so forth.

137
00:17:35,086 --> 00:17:41,740
So another example of a bond-breaking reaction is breaking the bond that holds proteins together.

138
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:43,460
It's called the peptide bond.

139
00:17:43,662 --> 00:17:44,580
And it's very stable.

140
00:17:44,840 --> 00:17:48,140
Proteins will last for a very long time in solution.

141
00:17:48,462 --> 00:17:49,700
So this is a stable bond.

142
00:17:50,574 --> 00:18:02,380
And Hojei Choi and Anqi Chen and Kejia Wu set out to design enzymes that cleave these bonds, and they were successful using a couple different chemistries.

143
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,720
And that was exciting because for two reasons.

144
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:13,180
First of all, this is definitely the hardest reaction that a completely designed from scratch protein had been able to do.

145
00:18:13,934 --> 00:18:22,720
Some of the data is shown on the right, this Anche's designs use a metal ion zinc, so they're only active in the presence of zinc.

146
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:30,980
So the protein that's getting cut is that band on the top of the gel, when the zinc is added gets cleaved, you see the lower band there.

147
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:40,540
We were also excited about this though, because there are many applications in therapeutics and elsewhere for proteins that cut proteins.

148
00:18:41,040 --> 00:18:43,560
And here's an example here.

149
00:18:43,560 --> 00:18:53,940
Another protein that's involved in neurodegenerative disease is called TDP-43, and it forms aggregates, as does tau, which I spoke about earlier.

150
00:18:54,380 --> 00:19:09,260
So Angie and Keija designed proteins that cleave TDP-43, and they're—I should also call out Don Hilvert, who's really been—I'm very, very lucky to have Don visit my lab every summer.

151
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:10,220
and it's been great fun.

152
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:14,400
and he's really been a guiding force for all the work we've been doing on enzyme design.

153
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:23,280
So this design is quite active, and it is also quite selective for its target.

154
00:19:23,726 --> 00:19:29,640
The reason proteases are interested therapeutically is each protease molecule can destroy many target molecules.

155
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:37,020
So if you take a drug, like an antibody, to block for a disease, what it's doing is it's blocking.

156
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:47,900
it's physically binding to a target, like the TNF receptor, I talked about at the beginning, and that requires a lot of the drug because you need as many copies.

157
00:19:47,900 --> 00:19:50,580
there are of the drug as the interactions you're blocking.

158
00:19:51,086 --> 00:19:58,220
The nice thing about a protease is each protease molecule can destroy many target molecules, so you can get by with much lower doses.

159
00:20:00,494 --> 00:20:02,520
Well, here's another bond-breaking reaction.

160
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:08,060
So in addition to toxins in the environment, we also have things like plastic and nylon that are accumulating.

161
00:20:08,500 --> 00:20:16,880
And this is work from Dongyo Kim and Jeremy Kub and Cesar, who have been designing proteins to break down nylon.

162
00:20:17,240 --> 00:20:23,500
So here we've been working with a fairly simple synthetic substrate that has the basic structure of nylon.

163
00:20:23,950 --> 00:20:27,660
We haven't been working with, you know, we haven't yet gotten to the point of taking nylon fabrics.

164
00:20:28,110 --> 00:20:38,160
But we have exciting results on, very recently, in being able to break this bond as well, and we're working on other plastics as well.

165
00:20:40,200 --> 00:20:45,410
Okay, so now on the flip side, making bonds.

166
00:20:46,040 --> 00:21:03,460
This is a very recent result with RFD4-proteina and it's a great collaboration with Anthony Green's lab and it's supported by the Gates Foundation and we're very lucky to have some of the people involved in this, Hans and John here today.

167
00:21:03,940 --> 00:21:14,700
And so here's what I'm showing you on the top is a step, a possible step in the synthesis of the HIV drug Lenacapivir.

168
00:21:15,342 --> 00:21:19,500
So the foundation and many others are very interested.

169
00:21:19,980 --> 00:21:24,580
Lenacapivir is a very potent anti-HIV drug, but it's very expensive to make.

170
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:26,220
It's a big, complicated molecule.

171
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:34,540
So we've been interested in the possibility of designing enzymes that would actually provide a new route to synthesizing lenacapavir.

172
00:21:35,020 --> 00:21:38,600
And the design protein is shown on the lower left.

173
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:52,100
This is work by Max Gantz and Saman Salekhi, and Nate Corley and Kieran designed, developed the RFD4 design methods I told you about, and Henry and Anthony did the test, the experimental test.

174
00:21:52,622 --> 00:22:07,440
So Max and Saman designed the sequences, and we sent them across the Atlantic to Manchester, and Henry and Anthony found that they were actually quite good enzymes on pretty much the first try with RFD4 protein, which we were very excited about.

175
00:22:07,822 --> 00:22:09,280
This shows some of Henry's data.

176
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:16,300
So first of all, on the bottom, you see what we want is the solid blue bar.

177
00:22:16,400 --> 00:22:22,800
And you can see when we add the enzyme, which is called 52 here, you can see we get a lot of activity without the enzyme.

178
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
The two parts just don't go together.

179
00:22:25,520 --> 00:22:32,300
And one of the things that's nice about enzymes is they have a lot of their, they have this, these enzymes have pretty defined pockets.

180
00:22:32,600 --> 00:22:44,040
So in this case, there's, there are two different versions of the molecules that they could be putting together and you just want one of them and separating out those different versions can be a very slow and painful step in drug synthesis.

181
00:22:44,430 --> 00:22:54,460
And here, this enzyme only puts together the two, the versions, in this case, the S enantiomer, as you can see above, it only uses the S enantiomer of one of the substrates.

182
00:22:55,118 --> 00:23:13,252
So we're very excited about this and sort of ushering in new routes to complex molecules, not only, well, certainly in the pharmaceutical industry and in global health, new ways of making molecules much more cheaply, but just more generally in chemical synthesis.

183
00:23:13,742 --> 00:23:22,080
There are many chemicals which we rely on today which require toxic solvents, which are very hard to make and can only be made inefficiently.

184
00:23:22,420 --> 00:23:28,440
So with designed enzymes, we are hopeful that we could come up with much greener, more effective ways to many of these molecules.

185
00:23:30,286 --> 00:23:36,300
Here is perhaps the most complex protein or enzyme that we've designed to date.

186
00:23:37,070 --> 00:23:41,900
This is an enzyme shown in blue that has binding sites for two different proteins.

187
00:23:42,446 --> 00:23:51,580
One protein called ubiquitin in green and the other protein in yellow is a particular target that this enzyme attaches ubiquitin to.

188
00:23:52,174 --> 00:24:05,020
So ubiquitin plays a key role in degrading disease-associated proteins, and the body and cells have very complex ways of targeting ubiquitins to specific residues.

189
00:24:05,454 --> 00:24:11,120
It's a big challenge for doing this chemically because the residue that gets attached to is a lysine.

190
00:24:11,260 --> 00:24:17,560
So ubiquitin gets attached to a lysine, and this protein, for example, is 16 different lysines.

191
00:24:18,040 --> 00:24:29,760
So the challenge was, could we do the complicated chemistry involved in transferring a ubiquitin to just to a lysine in the target in yellow and do this to just and just attach it exactly to just one lysine.

192
00:24:30,830 --> 00:24:37,840
And this is again, work by Salman and Max in collaboration with Max Futner and Catherine Long's lab.

193
00:24:38,862 --> 00:24:57,740
And so the results are really quite remarkable and this is work that this is from Max and Catherine that the design protein, indeed, this is PCNA, you see this band that's coming across top And only when we add the design protein, it adds ubiquitin quantitatively.

194
00:24:57,800 --> 00:24:59,120
We're getting complete addition.

195
00:24:59,502 --> 00:25:05,240
If we remove or if they remove just that one lysine that was getting attached, that completely goes away.

196
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:10,880
So this enzyme recognizes two proteins in just the right geometry and transfers the ubiquitin between them.

197
00:25:13,240 --> 00:25:19,900
And in sort of the reverse direction from polymer degradation, I talked about nylon.

198
00:25:20,900 --> 00:25:24,932
Here, this is work from Sean and Kate and Sam, I think Sam is here.

199
00:25:27,240 --> 00:25:31,340
And so here they took a small, this is sort of thinking about bioplastics.

200
00:25:31,440 --> 00:25:47,060
Can we take molecules that can be extracted from biological, say from waste or from other places, from biological matter and design an enzyme which will connect them into long polymers.

201
00:25:47,520 --> 00:26:02,660
Here we're starting with an activated monomer, or this is our first foray into this, but Sean and Kate have very exciting results already showing that they can take this one protein design, this one small molecule, and have an active site that just joins the molecule to itself to make polymers.

202
00:26:03,480 --> 00:26:09,940
Sean and Kate believe that they're actually getting much longer polymers in this, it's just that they don't stay soluble in solutions so they don't see them.

203
00:26:10,286 --> 00:26:18,116
So this would give a new route, instead of plastics made from petroleum, would give a route to plastics, plastic-like molecules made from biological feedstocks.

204
00:26:19,950 --> 00:26:22,760
And this is work by Florence Hardius here.

205
00:26:22,880 --> 00:26:24,980
This is a great collaboration with Anthony Green's lab.

206
00:26:25,500 --> 00:26:38,240
And here, Florence extended this idea of a designed active site to include a photosensitizer which is encoded as an unnatural amino acid, so expanding the amino acid alphabet.

207
00:26:38,940 --> 00:26:59,160
And in this case, she wanted to be able to control the stereoselectivity, so she has a design site that not only has this unnatural amino acid in the active site pocket, but it also has a shape which gives rise to a particular enantiomer of the product.

208
00:26:59,982 --> 00:27:15,080
And she, because this is all done from the ground up, starting from the active site, she could carry out design from both of, from one sort of ideal active site, or theozyme, and its mirror image.

209
00:27:15,080 --> 00:27:20,200
And this gives rise to products with the two different enantiomers.

210
00:27:21,220 --> 00:27:30,500
And so by building everything from the ground up, you can control selectivity in ways that are very hard to do, for example, by modifying existing enzymes.

211
00:27:31,182 --> 00:27:43,060
And the data is shown down below where the design that's only supposed to give the plus enantiomer only gives the plus enantiomer, and the one that's only supposed to give the minus enantiomer only gives the minus one.

212
00:27:44,400 --> 00:27:48,700
OK, so I'm going to tell you about one more class of enzymes.

213
00:27:49,102 --> 00:27:50,640
It's enzymes that work on DNA.

214
00:27:51,480 --> 00:28:05,080
And here are the first step, and this is work by Anisha Sehgal and Yulia Politansky, is what they set out to do is design proteins that could bind to very specific sequences of DNA.

215
00:28:05,678 --> 00:28:15,140
And so they designed here four different proteins, and each one of these proteins binds to a specific DNA sequence, 8 to 12 bases.

216
00:28:16,020 --> 00:28:35,660
And this is very interesting because there's a lot of these methodology is very exciting for being able to target transcriptional modification and genome modification, transcriptional activation and genome modification to specific sites, so for example, epigenetic modification.

217
00:28:36,160 --> 00:28:50,720
But the reason why I'm telling you about it here is Elliot, Riley, and Mia have fused to this a catalytic site that carries out a bond-breaking reaction, in this case, a DNA backbone-breaking reaction.

218
00:28:51,380 --> 00:28:57,960
And because it's all one rigid protein, these designs cut the DNA in a very specific site.

219
00:28:58,100 --> 00:28:59,320
They only cut at one site.

220
00:28:59,460 --> 00:29:14,740
So this gives sort of a new possibility for genome engineering and gene editing because is we can design enzymes now that will cut at it at only one specific spots, kind of like CRISPR-Cas9, but much, much smaller proteins, so perhaps easier to deliver.

221
00:29:16,302 --> 00:29:30,340
And then in the last few minutes, I'd like to show you some of the applications of protein design to more materials-like properties, because there are amazing materials in nature that are made out of proteins, things like silk, one of the strongest things that are known.

222
00:29:30,440 --> 00:29:32,340
It's just a protein, a string of proteins.

223
00:29:32,782 --> 00:29:44,300
And then there are other proteins that interact with earth-abundant minerals like calcium phosphate, calcium carbonate to make things like tooth and bone and shell.

224
00:29:44,440 --> 00:29:49,900
So we've been very interested in designing proteins with new material properties for a wide range of applications.

225
00:29:50,680 --> 00:30:01,900
So this is work by Wenjiao Dai, who designed a protein that to adopt using these sort of generative AI methods, a protein to design a crystal lattice.

226
00:30:02,340 --> 00:30:09,240
And what you see here is a system in which the protein's being made in an extract in vitro transcription translation system.

227
00:30:09,640 --> 00:30:20,080
And what's remarkable is while it's being made, it has such a strong tendency to crystallize, the crystal just starts forming right away as the protein's being made.

228
00:30:20,160 --> 00:30:21,800
So that was a real kind of aha moment.

229
00:30:24,390 --> 00:30:29,410
Nikita Hanakel has been working on the problem of lanthanide recovery.

230
00:30:30,010 --> 00:30:36,930
So lanthanides are used in many types of current technologies and they're very rare metals.

231
00:30:37,390 --> 00:30:42,330
And so there's a lot of interest in, and you can see the names at the bottom, you probably recognize a number of these.

232
00:30:42,690 --> 00:30:46,430
So there's a lot of interest in being able to recycle these or extract them.

233
00:30:47,374 --> 00:30:50,490
And that's very hard because they're very similar to each other.

234
00:30:50,930 --> 00:30:56,570
So Nikita has designed a series of proteins which can bind to individual lanthanides.

235
00:30:56,782 --> 00:31:00,450
And what you see these designs, A, B, through H, are different proteins.

236
00:31:00,910 --> 00:31:09,570
And yellow shows you, if you take a mix, the colors show you, if you take a mix of lanthanides, what that protein binds to best and how selective it is.

237
00:31:09,838 --> 00:31:22,530
So you see protein A is, the top one, is very specific for the lightest lanthanide, lanthanum, whereas the protein H is most specific for the heavier elements, ytterbium and lutetium.

238
00:31:23,230 --> 00:31:42,810
And so this gives sort of an exciting possibility for really contributing to the problem of lanthanide recycling which would be, could have significant impact on reducing the dependence on new mining for lanthanides and so forth.

239
00:31:44,526 --> 00:31:53,290
And then, finally, Tracy and Harley have been working on methods for promoting mineralization.

240
00:31:54,070 --> 00:32:09,590
And so the idea is if you take an inorganic mineral, in this case calcium phosphate, there's a bit of it shown on red on the left there, and they designed proteins which position groups to stabilize that lattice.

241
00:32:10,150 --> 00:32:12,270
And this is work by Tracy.

242
00:32:12,270 --> 00:32:15,390
She designed this protein tube you see here.

243
00:32:15,930 --> 00:32:22,170
And if you just incubate that tube on itself, you see the cryo-EM on the middle there.

244
00:32:22,290 --> 00:32:23,890
And it's sort of a hollow tube.

245
00:32:27,650 --> 00:32:31,730
After incubation with calcium phosphate precursors, you see the tube fills in.

246
00:32:32,078 --> 00:32:34,650
And you can see the density in the middle of the tube there.

247
00:32:35,150 --> 00:32:39,652
And if you look by EM, you see all these little hydroxyapatite crystals.

248
00:32:39,710 --> 00:32:39,940
cells.

249
00:32:40,110 --> 00:32:47,530
So this is not quite a tooth yet or a material like that, but it's a protein tube with hydroxyapatite on the inside.

250
00:32:48,190 --> 00:33:00,610
And Hanala Ruhola Baker's lab, Hanala's here, has shown that this protein, if it's made in the precursors to some of the early precursors to cells, actually forms these new types of structures in there.

251
00:33:00,718 --> 00:33:05,990
So it could be a route, part of a route eventually to artificial tooth-like material.

252
00:33:07,534 --> 00:33:13,090
So Tracy has used a similar approach to template the deposition of titanium oxide.

253
00:33:13,590 --> 00:33:24,790
So titanium oxide is a very interesting material, it's a photocatalyst among other things, but nature never really bothered doing anything with titanium oxide, it had no reason to.

254
00:33:25,210 --> 00:33:32,270
So again, the same idea to design a protein that templates, that's geometrically matched to a titanium oxide lattice.

255
00:33:33,010 --> 00:33:35,590
And here, again, you see this tube-like structure.

256
00:33:36,494 --> 00:33:40,890
This is a new design, which is sort of this barrel with these two halves.

257
00:33:42,670 --> 00:33:44,470
And Tracy designed it.

258
00:33:44,590 --> 00:33:49,124
It should have four sites where it nucleates titanium oxide.

259
00:33:49,350 --> 00:33:52,470
And you see, in the presence of titanium oxide, you see the four.

260
00:33:52,510 --> 00:33:53,950
In this image, you can really just see.

261
00:33:54,222 --> 00:33:58,630
You see that prominent dark area in the mineralized column.

262
00:33:58,650 --> 00:33:59,690
That's titanium oxide.

263
00:33:59,690 --> 00:34:15,429
So, we can now program where this mineral deposition is happening, and we're very excited about this for a number of applications, including patterning semiconductor materials, where patterning things at ever finer times, length scales getting harder and harder.

264
00:34:16,045 --> 00:34:18,909
And you see here the titanium oxide crystals on the right.

265
00:34:20,462 --> 00:34:27,810
And then finally, I've told you about conformational change, like in the plant defense systems.

266
00:34:28,078 --> 00:34:47,870
I've told you about catalysis, and where we're going now is to put these things together to make protein machines, because many of the most sophisticated functions in nature are done by proteins, which cycle through different states, driven by either the light energy or a chemical fuel energy.

267
00:34:48,230 --> 00:34:52,350
And I'm just going to give you one example today, and this is work by Catherine Shelley and cholinergists.

268
00:34:52,790 --> 00:34:57,027
So this is a hinge-like protein, which has two states.

269
00:34:57,582 --> 00:35:00,644
You can see the protein kind of bends like this.

270
00:35:01,090 --> 00:35:05,050
And the neat thing about this system is it can be toggled with light.

271
00:35:05,190 --> 00:35:13,490
And you can see what's happening in this zigzag here is light at one wavelength is being shown, and then light at a different wavelength.

272
00:35:13,630 --> 00:35:16,650
And the system can be pushed back and forth between the different states.

273
00:35:17,230 --> 00:35:24,030
And I'm not showing it here, but in the different states, this protein binds differentially with other proteins in the environment.

274
00:35:24,210 --> 00:35:28,550
So we can cycle it, its interactions with proteins around it.

275
00:35:28,690 --> 00:35:32,670
And so that's sort of the fundamental thing you need to make a biological machine.

276
00:35:33,210 --> 00:35:38,510
So we're currently in other work that I'm not showing you about doing the same thing with chemical fuels.

277
00:35:38,830 --> 00:35:42,770
And one of the first things we're trying to do is make proteins that can actually walk along a track.

278
00:35:44,622 --> 00:35:48,730
So those were some of the things we can do with protein design today.

279
00:35:48,730 --> 00:35:57,550
And I just wanted to close, first of all, by thanking the amazing people in my group who have done this work.

280
00:35:58,130 --> 00:36:04,090
And this picture's taken actually 12, like most of this talk, it came together yesterday.

281
00:36:04,730 --> 00:36:08,370
And people sent slides, and then I got everyone to take a picture.

282
00:36:08,590 --> 00:36:11,050
So you're getting up-to-date stuff.

283
00:36:11,170 --> 00:36:15,030
And so I've sort of outlined the different applications of being able to program MATTER.

284
00:36:15,030 --> 00:36:18,690
My colleague, Neil King, is working on vaccines for global health.

285
00:36:18,958 --> 00:36:21,130
I've talked about cancer therapies.

286
00:36:21,470 --> 00:36:22,950
We're very excited about machines

287
00:36:27,342 --> 00:36:34,470
for preventing buildup of things in circulation, which could help with problems like regeneration and longevity.

288
00:36:36,490 --> 00:36:45,668
Diagnostics, I talked about with ImmunoTow, and I showed you the example with immunotherapies of the cancer therapeutic that was equally effective, but much less toxic.

289
00:36:46,970 --> 00:36:50,870
I didn't talk about it today, but we're doing a lot of work on chemical sensors.

290
00:36:51,470 --> 00:36:54,710
I've showed you some of the new types of materials we're hoping to be able to make.

291
00:36:56,430 --> 00:37:01,956
And I didn't talk about today, but maybe the next time we're working on protein-based computing systems.

292
00:37:02,710 --> 00:37:11,130
And then finally, on sustainability, I talked about destroying organophosphates and degrading nylon.

293
00:37:11,690 --> 00:37:19,250
I talked about the design of plant defense systems and green chemistry.

294
00:37:19,430 --> 00:37:23,790
We're very excited about being able to design complex molecules like Glenacapavir.

295
00:37:24,526 --> 00:37:30,230
So I want to thank again the amazing people who did this work and thank you for your attention.

296
00:37:30,350 --> 00:37:31,530
I'd be happy to take questions.

297
00:37:48,870 --> 00:37:50,790
David, thank you for a wonderful talk.

298
00:37:51,450 --> 00:37:55,530
Maybe I could start the questions just by asking you, where are the limits here?

299
00:37:55,690 --> 00:37:59,130
What, are there compounds that you can't target?

300
00:37:59,350 --> 00:38:06,430
There's a big story in the news today about PFAS polyfluoroalkylated substances.

301
00:38:07,086 --> 00:38:11,770
I mean, would they be potential targets of the sort of technology that you're talking about?

302
00:38:11,770 --> 00:38:26,830
Yeah, so there are two postdocs in my group now working on PFAS degradation, but that's a very good, when you bring up limitations, the problem, so PFAS compounds are forever chemicals and they're for amber chemicals because they're very stable, they're very hard to break.

303
00:38:27,210 --> 00:38:36,610
And so I think we're up in chemistry, we're up against challenges like that, where the intrinsic chemistry is the activation barrier to breaking those bonds is very, very high.

304
00:38:37,030 --> 00:38:38,930
And so we have not succeeded that way.

305
00:38:39,090 --> 00:38:45,710
There we've, we're focusing for now on easier compounds, chlorinated compounds rather than fluorinated compounds, but we're hoping to get there.

306
00:38:45,930 --> 00:38:54,210
But I think that's in terms of the answer to your question, I think the limits that we would be up against are, well, to some extent, we're limited by our imaginations.

307
00:38:54,862 --> 00:38:58,330
Like, okay, so I showed you we can make new materials, but what should we make materials for?

308
00:38:58,930 --> 00:39:04,330
And, you know, what minerals would be most interesting to combine with them?

309
00:39:04,686 --> 00:39:08,190
But we're also up against some physical limitations, like in the PFAS case.

310
00:39:08,590 --> 00:39:16,450
I think one of the challenges with proteins, if you want to solve modern day problems facing humans, is the problem of scale.

311
00:39:16,730 --> 00:39:23,010
So you can make a kilogram of a protein if you really try, making a thousand tons of protein is hard.

312
00:39:23,370 --> 00:39:25,870
So that's why catalysts are particularly interesting.

313
00:39:27,150 --> 00:39:35,330
But from a technical point of view, I think things are advancing very quickly, so it's really hard to say that there is an insurmountable challenge.

314
00:39:35,750 --> 00:39:41,370
One of the things that probably many people are thinking about is the immunogenicity of design proteins when you put them into the body.

315
00:39:42,870 --> 00:39:51,230
There, we're working on methods to mitigate that, and we haven't seen large responses, but like any time you put a foreign protein in the body, that could be a challenge.

316
00:39:51,550 --> 00:39:54,830
So there are a variety of challenges, I would say.

317
00:39:55,230 --> 00:39:55,410
Right.

318
00:39:55,710 --> 00:39:56,690
Thank you very much.

319
00:39:56,930 --> 00:39:57,350
Questions?

320
00:39:58,430 --> 00:39:58,610
Yep.

321
00:39:59,086 --> 00:39:59,850
In the front there.

322
00:40:01,774 --> 00:40:04,790
So the microphone will come down here.

323
00:40:05,010 --> 00:40:06,788
It's winging its way towards you.

324
00:40:10,560 --> 00:40:11,000
Oh, it's Brian.

325
00:40:11,380 --> 00:40:11,540
Yeah.

326
00:40:11,840 --> 00:40:13,180
Brilliant lecture.

327
00:40:13,180 --> 00:40:26,244
What I want to ask you is, people like you probably speculate or have speculated why evolution limited the number of amino acids that are coded for, whether a four-base codon might have given more versatility.

328
00:40:27,060 --> 00:40:34,948
Does your work give you an answer to that, and do you limit yourself to natural amino acids, and do unnatural amino acids get around the immunogenicity problem?

329
00:40:36,540 --> 00:40:43,080
Well, as far as why nature stopped at 20, you know, it's a little hard to ask, I mean.

330
00:40:46,720 --> 00:40:55,620
But you can clearly, I think probably part of the answer there is that, you know, proteins do an amazing, carry out an amazing range of functions with just the 20 amino acids.

331
00:40:56,260 --> 00:40:59,620
Protein design can be enhanced by using unnatural amino acids.

332
00:40:59,662 --> 00:41:10,160
and I showed the example from Florence's work where she introduced a natural amino acid that enabled us to do photochemistry that wasn't possible with the natural amino acids.

333
00:41:10,620 --> 00:41:21,420
And so I think expanding beyond 20 amino acids will enable incorporation of functions where that protein's on their own, the 20 amino acids can't do.

334
00:41:21,838 --> 00:41:33,500
As far as evading immunogenicity, certainly that's probably going to be most true when you have many amino acids, many unnatural amino acids, for example, many D-amino acids.

335
00:41:34,140 --> 00:41:38,660
And so, for example, in macrocyclic peptides, there becomes a manufacturing problem.

336
00:41:38,780 --> 00:41:42,480
Like, how do you actually make a protein with many, many unnatural amino acids?

337
00:41:42,798 --> 00:41:46,940
So my colleague, Gaurav Bhardwaj at the Institute for Protein Design is working on that.

338
00:41:49,134 --> 00:41:49,460
Thanks.

339
00:41:49,680 --> 00:41:50,880
Over at the side there.

340
00:41:53,390 --> 00:41:53,590
Hi.

341
00:41:54,690 --> 00:41:57,690
I saw somewhat, perhaps, following on from the last question.

342
00:41:57,690 --> 00:42:01,450
And I guess biology is not restricted to the amino acids.

343
00:42:01,610 --> 00:42:04,610
There's also glycans and also lipids.

344
00:42:04,710 --> 00:42:13,670
So I was wondering sort of these, I guess, over the years have been considered sort of side players, but we now know that they play a very important role.

345
00:42:13,770 --> 00:42:19,510
So I was wondering, do they, in terms of Lego bricks that you have, do they also come in soon?

346
00:42:19,890 --> 00:42:23,870
Yeah, so with carbohydrates, there's been some exciting recent project.

347
00:42:23,870 --> 00:42:37,350
In fact, Neil King and I have a joint student, Meg, who Meg's found that she can design glycoproteins where she can control the amount of processing of the sugar just by controlling the accessibility of it to the processing apparatus.

348
00:42:37,970 --> 00:42:43,790
So sugars are now a legitimate part of the design toolkit.

349
00:42:46,126 --> 00:42:46,460
Thank you.

350
00:42:46,734 --> 00:42:47,740
One in the middle there.

351
00:42:47,940 --> 00:42:48,020
Yeah.

352
00:42:48,300 --> 00:42:55,300
So in case of de novo design of therapeutic proteins, we currently see success rates of about 10, 20%?

353
00:42:55,918 --> 00:42:59,300
What do we need to improve to increase the success rates?

354
00:42:59,920 --> 00:43:01,740
Well, it really depends on the challenge.

355
00:43:01,940 --> 00:43:09,260
So for example, if you're trying to design PFAS degrading proteins, you'll be overjoyed at a 10% success rate.

356
00:43:10,542 --> 00:43:21,680
Whereas for designing proteins that bind to just a static protein and binds a static target, there the success rates can be upwards of 70%.

357
00:43:21,966 --> 00:43:24,540
So it all depends on the difficulty of the problem.

358
00:43:25,220 --> 00:43:37,180
And what we've seen over the last few years as our generative design, AI design methods have gotten better, is that the success rates have just kept going up.

359
00:43:37,260 --> 00:43:44,960
And so I showed you that RFD4 protein result, where the success rates are considerably higher than our previous models.

360
00:43:45,040 --> 00:43:46,400
And I think that will continue.

361
00:43:46,400 --> 00:44:04,120
we and and part of this is understanding it's it's better able to do to better optimize designs and then to get more experimental feedback to understand what properties are really critical for experimental success.

362
00:44:04,500 --> 00:44:14,860
So I anticipate the success on the easier problems will continue to increase and of course we hope that carries over to increasing increasing on the success on in the very hard problems like PFAS degradation.

363
00:44:16,014 --> 00:44:16,320
Thank you.

364
00:44:16,718 --> 00:44:17,400
Yep, one there.

365
00:44:21,294 --> 00:44:22,640
Thank you for a wonderful talk.

