00:44:22,990 --> 00:44:29,940
I was wondering about the solution conditions in which the protein structures and the protein interactions are modeled in.

367
00:44:30,382 --> 00:44:37,520
Do you think there would be an effect of the solution conditions in terms of solution ions and other parameters on those two properties?

368
00:44:38,200 --> 00:44:46,960
And I was also wondering if you could please comment on if and how you modeled to be similar to the cellular environment?

369
00:44:47,220 --> 00:44:48,340
Yeah, that's a good question.

370
00:44:48,814 --> 00:45:01,180
I would say to a first approximation, the design proteins I showed you are not sensitive to the variations in salt concentration and pH that occur under normal physiological conditions.

371
00:45:01,518 --> 00:45:06,620
Where that's become important is when we want to design switches that, for example, respond to pH.

372
00:45:07,022 --> 00:45:18,240
So the tumor microenvironment also has a lower pH, and so we want things that will turn on, or in other cases, we want things that will release in the endosome where the pH is lower.

373
00:45:18,700 --> 00:45:29,880
And so there we can explicitly design in structural elements like hydrogen bonds to histine residues that are pH sensitive where the interaction either becomes stronger or weaker when the pH changes.

374
00:45:30,580 --> 00:45:47,200
So I would say that by and large, the design proteins are not sensitive to variation or the physiological range, but it's a very exciting design challenge to make proteins that are sensitive So, you can have them active when you want them and not when you don't want them.

375
00:45:49,038 --> 00:45:49,240
Yep.

376
00:45:49,660 --> 00:45:49,780
On

377
00:45:58,550 --> 00:46:09,092
that previous point around, you know, designing with various conformational switches in mind, where are we with being able to incorporate things like de novo design into logic gating for things like CAR T therapy?

378
00:46:10,094 --> 00:46:15,290
Yeah, well, that is one of the main places where I think where design switches will be useful.

379
00:46:15,850 --> 00:46:20,870
So, we're working now on switchable sort of designed logic for CAR-T cell therapy.

380
00:46:21,490 --> 00:46:30,350
So for example, you want to be able to sense exhaustion and then have that respond to something that will make the cell less susceptible to exhaustion.

381
00:46:30,590 --> 00:46:31,990
So that's one of the things we're doing.

382
00:46:32,494 --> 00:46:40,930
Likewise, for recognition, having sort of AND gate logic so that you only kill cells that have two surface markers.

383
00:46:41,326 --> 00:46:43,510
So those are both current research efforts.

384
00:46:43,510 --> 00:46:44,470
Which

385
00:46:48,560 --> 00:46:53,560
is about the computational resources that you need for these problems.

386
00:46:53,560 --> 00:47:01,780
So, we've been hearing about mathematicians and solving mathematical challenges with huge amounts of computer.

387
00:47:01,780 --> 00:47:05,540
So, are you confronted with the same challenge?

388
00:47:05,540 --> 00:47:06,540
Well,

389
00:47:06,540 --> 00:47:15,260
the the difference between mathematics and biology is that mathematics, you know, that the things can be can be shown.

390
00:47:15,260 --> 00:47:22,860
For example, when you train a language model, for example, on maths problems, you absolutely know the answer, right?

391
00:47:23,060 --> 00:47:28,600
And so you can give an essentially unlimited number of problems and keep training the model to get better and better at solving problems.

392
00:47:28,878 --> 00:47:32,160
And some of those solutions can then take a very large amount of time.

393
00:47:32,440 --> 00:47:38,360
And training those models is something that only a company can do, because it's too expensive.

394
00:47:40,270 --> 00:47:49,700
The training, the models that I described here, where these models, I should say, are trained on the protein structures and the protein data bank, by and large.

395
00:47:49,800 --> 00:47:59,160
And so, really, this is all lying on the work of, you know, the, you know, generations of crystallographers who solved all those structures in the database and the curators.

396
00:47:59,694 --> 00:48:08,360
But all in all, it's less data, and we don't have the same sort of thing with verified with being able to create synthetic verifiable data that you have now.

397
00:48:08,398 --> 00:48:13,680
So, the training sets are smaller, likewise the inference is faster.

398
00:48:14,120 --> 00:48:25,460
So it does take compute for us to train these models, but that compute is many, many orders of magnitude less than it takes to train the language models.

399
00:48:26,040 --> 00:48:29,540
And then to actually use the models takes much less compute.

400
00:48:30,060 --> 00:48:38,360
So it's generally within the realm of any lab that has some kind of access to modern computers.

401
00:48:38,620 --> 00:48:44,500
So I would say there's less of this feeling of angst about we can't do what we need to do.

402
00:48:44,780 --> 00:48:44,920
Okay.

403
00:48:45,358 --> 00:48:45,580
Good.

404
00:48:47,278 --> 00:48:47,600
Any more?

405
00:48:47,680 --> 00:48:48,900
One in the front here.

406
00:48:49,260 --> 00:48:49,380
Yeah.

407
00:48:53,518 --> 00:48:54,360
Thank you so much.

408
00:48:54,460 --> 00:48:55,380
The talk was very interesting.

409
00:48:55,780 --> 00:48:56,420
A quick question.

410
00:48:56,660 --> 00:48:57,720
I have heard

411
00:48:57,720 --> 00:49:10,520
from other people in the field, especially in other structure prediction work in the UK, that they're looking at data augmentation using predicted structures or predicted MD trajectories, et cetera.

412
00:49:10,520 --> 00:49:17,640
What is your opinion on that, because it seems a bit weird to, you know, use predicted data to predict more data.

413
00:49:17,940 --> 00:49:23,060
Yeah, no, that's a very good question, and there shouldn't be a free lunch, so, right.

414
00:49:23,260 --> 00:49:31,000
So what we find is that if there is something being put in that's, if it's not purely synthetic, then it can impact.

415
00:49:31,180 --> 00:49:45,100
So one of the things that we found following, actually, DeepMind's lead was that if you took sequences that you knew were likely to fold, because they were present in a biological system, and you predicted their structures.

416
00:49:45,900 --> 00:49:59,260
You could do this on a very large scale, for example, take metagenome sequences and predict the structures, and then train on those, and do a lot better, and improve your model, and we could quantify what that improvement was, and it was pretty marked.

417
00:50:00,846 --> 00:50:07,260
But that's not completely synthetic data, because we use the fact that those sequences probably fold to some protein.

418
00:50:07,630 --> 00:50:11,840
If you generate completely random sequences and fold those, that would probably be less useful.

419
00:50:14,094 --> 00:50:15,420
One right at the back there.

420
00:50:16,494 --> 00:50:17,820
Is this an online question?

421
00:50:18,160 --> 00:50:20,860
Yes, this is a question from our online audience.

422
00:50:21,520 --> 00:50:35,560
So given the longevity of these proteins and producing novel proteins we don't see naturally, what is the potential impact of these in the environment and on plants slash animals, e.g. post-medicinal use in humans going into sewage?

423
00:50:35,560 --> 00:50:41,060
Yeah, so one of the things about proteins is they are the ultimate biodegradable stuff.

424
00:50:41,262 --> 00:50:49,340
I mean, you know, we're all eating proteins all the time and there are things in the environment that are munging up protein and scavenging them for amino acids.

425
00:50:49,870 --> 00:51:02,320
So even though the proteins we design are stable, they will all be cut up into amino acids and recycled into new proteins, you know, either in the environment or within the body of whoever is, you know, anyone's getting those.

426
00:51:02,382 --> 00:51:10,360
So that is one of the nice thing about proteins is they can do all these incredibly sophisticated functions, but they're completely biodegradable.

427
00:51:13,120 --> 00:51:13,260
Yeah.

428
00:51:13,640 --> 00:51:13,940
Jane.

429
00:51:16,814 --> 00:51:17,280
Thank you.

430
00:51:18,260 --> 00:51:23,108
In biology, often things depend on kinetic sort of lifetimes of complexes.

431
00:51:24,078 --> 00:51:26,200
Have you been able to do anything where you can

432
00:51:26,200 --> 00:51:26,800
sort

433
00:51:26,800 --> 00:51:27,920
of manipulate lifetimes?

434
00:51:27,982 --> 00:51:33,560
Because it seems to me that most of what you're doing is saying, bind hard and bind long until something happens.

435
00:51:33,720 --> 00:51:35,044
You know, let's bind it, take it out.

436
00:51:36,078 --> 00:51:41,420
Have you thought about sort of manipulating lifetimes, you could manipulate networks in cells?

437
00:51:41,500 --> 00:51:47,780
Well, the closest thing to that that I talked about was the IL-2 mimic with the off switch.

438
00:51:49,100 --> 00:51:54,560
So it's a little bit like, you know, the serpents that get made and then they turn off.

439
00:51:54,680 --> 00:52:01,920
Here we designed that the protein works, it activates the immune system, and then when you add the off switch, you turn it off.

440
00:52:01,920 --> 00:52:06,120
So you essentially shorten its effective lifetime.

441
00:52:06,880 --> 00:52:10,160
And so that's one type of control mechanism.

442
00:52:10,606 --> 00:52:15,280
In terms of designing proteins that first fold to one thing and then fold another one, that's harder.

443
00:52:15,680 --> 00:52:30,040
But another thing one can do with the design of proteases and the design of functions, you can design proteins that have meant to have a particular lifetime because they get degraded either by a natural protease or by a design protease over some period of time.

444
00:52:32,560 --> 00:52:36,640
Yep, we've got two more questions, one in the middle there.

445
00:52:38,300 --> 00:52:38,600
Thanks.

446
00:52:39,120 --> 00:52:40,560
Truly inspiring talk.

447
00:52:40,700 --> 00:52:41,460
I was wondering,

448
00:52:41,460 --> 00:52:45,380
you mentioned here and there things like immunogenicity, stability.

449
00:52:46,126 --> 00:53:00,868
and I guess there is a longer list of properties like solubility or manufacturability or specificity when it comes to binding, that can have an important role in translation for this technology.

450
00:53:01,102 --> 00:53:11,812
So, do you think these properties emerge from the design, from the training data, do you think they should be assessed during translation, or I just wonder what's the view of the…

451
00:53:11,840 --> 00:53:19,920
Yeah, I think the, well, the question is, you know, I told you that design proteins are almost always quite stable and are made at pretty high amounts in bacteria, for example.

452
00:53:20,430 --> 00:53:28,000
I think it's, again, they have, the models have been trained on the protein structure data bank, which are all the nicely folded proteins that we know about.

453
00:53:28,302 --> 00:53:40,400
And so they've learned good habits, you know, and they also, naturally occurring proteins often have very each, you know, they have these idiosyncratic features that just happen to happen because of evolutionary drift or whatever.

454
00:53:40,820 --> 00:53:49,060
The design protein, the models sort of don't pick up on those because they're not common to many proteins in the training set.

455
00:53:49,180 --> 00:53:53,540
So the proteins that are generated tend to be a little bit more idealized.

456
00:53:53,966 --> 00:53:56,120
They don't have as many odd things about their structure.

457
00:53:56,120 --> 00:54:16,000
As far as immunogenicity, so we've been incorporating into our design methods knowledge of MHC binding preferences so we can specifically avoid peptides that bind to class 1 or class 2 MHCs.

458
00:54:16,750 --> 00:54:21,620
So that will be one way to design molecules which should be less immunogenic.

459
00:54:24,750 --> 00:54:25,080
Thank you.

460
00:54:25,080 --> 00:54:27,340
Final question there, and yeah.

461
00:54:28,430 --> 00:54:29,820
You talked a lot about making proteins.

462
00:54:30,030 --> 00:54:32,060
Also, you want kilograms, maybe even more.

463
00:54:32,220 --> 00:54:35,980
I mean, are you sometimes frustrated that you need ribosomes to do that all so complicated?

464
00:54:36,142 --> 00:54:37,156
Is there better ways of doing it?

465
00:54:37,900 --> 00:54:50,840
Well, we're working on sort of a ribosome-free route for a protein synthesis, but mainly so we can make molecules that aren't just all natural amino acids and aren't even all put together by peptide bonds.

466
00:54:50,900 --> 00:54:59,600
so just generalized polymers, where the idea is, you have a template, which in our case, —this is a peptide, long peptide, that basically has a code that tells you what monomers to put together.

467
00:55:00,760 --> 00:55:09,860
But in terms of, I think, you know, it tends to be kind of hard to do, to improve on nature where nature spent a lot of time trying to figure it out.

468
00:55:09,960 --> 00:55:12,320
And so the ribosome is really kind of a mirror.

469
00:55:12,440 --> 00:55:18,400
If you want to make polypeptides from the 20 amino acids, it's going to be a, well, you asked about another challenge.

470
00:55:18,640 --> 00:55:25,040
Doing better, outdoing nature on something that nature spent 2 billion years perfecting is hard to do.

471
00:55:25,480 --> 00:55:29,700
Although, I think for things like rubisco, you know, rubisco is not the world's greatest enzyme.

472
00:55:29,902 --> 00:55:34,380
I think there's hope, but yes, we would love to be able to improve on the ribosome.

473
00:55:34,500 --> 00:55:36,920
I think that goes into the very hard problem category.

